<DocsLayout title="Team access" activeNav="/platform/team-access">
  <h1>Team access</h1>
  <p>
    Team access controls who can reach a workspace and what they can do in it. It also holds two
    workspace-wide settings: the display name, and whether new credential forms check for
    duplicates.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="team-access"
    alt="Team access screen with four members and their roles, workspace settings, and one pending invitation"
    caption="Members, roles, workspace settings, and pending invitations on one screen."
  />

  <h2>Roles</h2>
  <p>
    There are four roles, and each is a fixed set of permissions rather than something you compose:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Permission</th>
        <th>Owner</th>
        <th>Admin</th>
        <th>Contributor</th>
        <th>Viewer</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>View the workspace</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Manage credentials</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Manage applications</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Manage the team</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Manage integrations</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Run pipelines</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Close the workspace</td><td>✓</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Two consequences are easy to miss:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Contributors can manage credentials but not applications.</strong> The role is shaped
      for someone who maintains signing material without restructuring the workspace.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Closing the workspace is the owner's alone.</strong> Admins can do everything else,
      including managing members.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The <a href="/platform/audit-log">audit log</a> is stricter still: only owners and admins can
    open it.
  </p>

  <h2>Inviting someone</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Invite member</strong> takes an email and a role and sends an invitation that expires.
    Pending invitations are listed on the right with their role and expiry, and can be revoked before
    they are accepted.
  </p>
  <p>
    An invitation is tied to the email it was sent to. The recipient accepts it from their own
    account, which is why inviting someone does not require them to exist in the system first.
  </p>

  <h2>Changing a role or removing a member</h2>
  <p>
    Roles are edited inline from the member row — the change takes effect on their next request, not
    at their next sign-in. Removing a member revokes their access to this workspace only; their
    account and their other memberships are untouched.
  </p>
  <p>
    The owner row has no role selector: ownership is not something another member can reassign from
    this screen.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both actions are recorded in the audit log as <code>team.member.role_changed</code> and
    <code>team.member.removed</code>, and both are available as
    <a href="/platform/integrations/webhooks">webhook events</a> if you mirror access changes
    elsewhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Roles and API keys are separate gates</h2>
  <p>
    An <a href="/platform/api-keys">API key</a> carries its own scopes, but it also inherits the
    limits of the member it belongs to. A key with <code>credentials:write</code> held by a viewer
    still cannot write credentials. When a request fails with <code>403</code> and the scope looks
    correct, the role is usually the reason.
  </p>
  <p>
    This also means demoting a member quietly narrows what their keys can do — worth remembering
    when a pipeline starts failing after a role change.
  </p>

  <h2>Workspace settings on this screen</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Workspace name</td>
        <td>The display name every member sees. The slug in URLs does not change with it.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Credential uniqueness</td>
        <td>
          When on, new credential forms look for an existing credential of the same type with the
          same file and identifying fields, and warn before creating a duplicate. See
          <a href="/platform/credentials-vault">Credentials Vault</a>.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>No API for membership</h2>
  <p>
    Members, roles, and invitations are console-only. There is no API v1 endpoint and no CLI
    command, so access changes are deliberately a human action rather than something a pipeline can
    perform.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
